WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.130
Imagine you're running a small business.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:08.900
You borrow some money.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.220
You're paying it back.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.520
Maybe you've fallen
behind, maybe you haven't.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.720
And then one day out of the blue,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.543
you can't get any of your
money out of the bank.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.930
Your accounts are locked up.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.053
They've been seized
without any notice to you.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.710
This is happening to small business people

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.423
across America every day.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.810
And it's enabled by an obscure document

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:37.653
called a confession of judgment.

00:00:37.653 --> 00:00:41.550
It just kept getting worse,
there was no money left.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:45.630
I was ready to take my life
to clear everybody else.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.730
I'm Zach Mider, I'm a
reporter at Bloomberg News.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:52.090
I'm Zeke Faux, I'm a
reporter for Bloomberg News

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.240
and Business Week Magazine.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.890
And we're the ones who wrote

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.340
Sign Here to Lose Everything.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.500
This is an extraordinary
period for America's economy.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.884
We've seen triple digit
swings in the stock market.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.270
Major financial institutions have teetered

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.843
on the edge of collapse
and some have failed.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.540
After the financial crisis a decade ago,

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.830
a lot of small businesses needed loans

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.223
and they couldn't always get
them from traditional banks.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.130
Instead people who stepped in were called

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.663
cash advance companies.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.700
These guys call businesses
across the country

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.300
and they're offering quick
money and they say that

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.270
if you're in tight spot they
can get you 50, a hundred

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.080
grand overnight.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.910
The loans are very expensive,
with interest rates as high as

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:00.120
300 or 500 percent annualized.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.963
That's 20 times as much as
you'd pay on a credit card.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.690
The merchant cash advance
industry has attracted

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.600
all sorts of characters
because of the potential

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.420
to make a quick profit.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.230
So there are cash advance
brokers with convictions for

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.420
drug smuggling, stock fraud.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.100
If you go and talk to
someone in the shady side

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.980
of Wall Street, the odds
are, they either work

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.080
in cash advance, or they've considered it.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.200
One of the biggest companies

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.470
in the cash advance space
is Yellowstone Capital

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.223
and it was actually founded
by this guy David Glass.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:41.650
I didn't get to where I am today

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.730
by losing my client's money.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.110
Glass actually served as the inspiration

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:50.960
for the classic stock
scam movie Boiler Room.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.210
You call me when the
stock doubles alright?

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.310
Glass heard about this cash advance boom

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.970
and started Yellowstone.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.330
And it quickly grew to
be one of the biggest

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.933
cash advance brokerages.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.300
They're lending to small businesses.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.423
Picture like a plumber, an
electrician, or a shopkeeper.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.100
But they realize quickly
that some businesses

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.120
were taking loans and then just

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.460
stealing the money essentially.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.970
Like they had no intention
of paying it back.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:28.290
So a lawyer, working for
Yellowstone and some other

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.820
cash advance companies, had
an idea that they could revive

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.513
this age old practice called
confessions of judgment.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.740
A confession of judgment is something

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.320
that the borrower signs as a
condition of getting the loan.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.650
It basically means they
give up their right

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:47.713
to be heard in court.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.220
So later on if they miss a payment,

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.770
or even if the lender wants to
claim they missed a payment,

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:57.930
it doesn't have to be true,
the lender can go to court

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.220
with this confession of judgment,
which gives them the power

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.143
to raid the borrower's bank account.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.440
Confessions of judgment aren't
even valid in many states.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.913
But New York state law
allows this practice.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.410
The borrowers are all across the country.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:18.610
Cleveland, or Topeka,
Kansas, or Los Angeles,

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.750
but typically they're all required to sign

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.003
a confession of judgment that's
valid in a New York court.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.320
When you stop and think about it,

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.150
it's crazy that a borrower
would admit in advance

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.220
to not paying back a loan,
when they haven't even

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:35.820
received the money yet.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.840
It was so illogical, I
really wanted to know more.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.390
We thought about, uh maybe
we should do a story,

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:42.780
but we didn't have a sense of the scale.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:44.830
Like is this something that's used a lot?

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:50.090
And we ultimately found
like more than 25,000

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.083
of these judgments filed
in the last coupla years.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.170
They were being used to go after borrowers

00:04:58.170 --> 00:04:59.820
all over the country.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.960
And Yellowstone was filing these things

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.663
at a rate of like several a day.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.360
This system has no safeguards.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.090
We found lots of borrowers who said that

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.300
confessions of judgment
were filed against them

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.090
when it was not warranted,
or the cash advance company

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.470
seized more money than they were owed.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.570
We found, after talking to
borrowers, that a lot of them

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.770
had had their lives turned upside down

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.053
by confessions of judgment.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.310
My name is Jerry Bush, Jr.,
I basically was a plumber

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:35.813
for about 30 years.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.440
Jerry Bush ran a plumbing
business with his father,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.540
near Roanoke, Virginia.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.370
And the company got a lot of jobs,

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.240
but he had cash flow problems

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.590
and he started taking these cash advances.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.600
The confession of judgment
that was part of the contract

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.280
that you had to sign and they
have very high interest rates,

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.520
but we needed money.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.300
So he kept taking more
and more of these loans

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.930
in order to get the money to
pay the loans he already had.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:17.440
It got to be so bad that he
was paying back $18,000 a day

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:18.780
at one point.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:22.220
So I hada close doors
because there was no way

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.120
I could make the payments.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.990
The way these contracts work,
the cash advance companies

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:30.470
shouldn't be able to go
after someone to collect

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:31.923
if their business fails.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:36.890
But because Jerry had signed
these confessions of judgment

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:39.860
the cash advance companies went after him.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.070
They got my father's retirement money.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:44.860
They took it out of my son's
account, which he was 17,

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.350
but they said my name was on the account.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.203
So they took all his savings.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:52.160
I kept gettin' calls.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.280
One funding company told me,
he said you have two ways out.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:58.540
Win a lottery, or die.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.820
I took the chance and I
was ready to take my life

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.313
to clear everybody else.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:08.820
He took a lot of pain pills,
walked out into the woods

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:13.800
near his house and recorded a
video in which he said goodbye

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.973
to his son and wife.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.193
They found him and took
him to the hospital.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:23.963
I'm survivin' every day.

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:27.100
My hope is that they will stop these

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.080
confessions of judgments.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.850
I write to senators,
Congress, almost every week

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:34.803
and I'm just hopin' we
can get some laws passed.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.120
After we filed the story
in November of 2018,

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:52.203
we got quite a lot of response
from government officials.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.340
On the federal level, members
of Congress in both chambers

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:01.250
have filed legislation
to end the practice of

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.503
confession of judgment nationwide.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.170
In New York, the state
Attorney General has opened

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:12.060
an investigation and among the
companies they're looking at

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:13.853
are Yellowstone Capital.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.100
This is a story that needed
a light shone on it actively

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:20.253
by the press.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:25.420
If we hadn't published it
when we did, you wouldn't see

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:28.800
the official reaction that you see now.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.160
But while that's going on,
people are still having

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:34.823
confessions of judgment
filed against them everyday.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.480
If you look up Yellowstone
Capital, you can see their

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.380
salesmen on Instagram
posting pictures of their

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:50.023
Lamborghinis, diamond
watches, fancy vacations,

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:52.803
so they seem to be doing great.

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:59.420
Even if confessions of judgment go away,

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.090
there are all kinds of
other abusive practices

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:04.140
in the cash advance industry.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:07.120
From brokers signing
customers up for loans

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.520
they don't really want, to
lenders tryin' to collect money

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:12.910
they were never owed.

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:15.810
So you're probably gonna be
hearing more about this industry

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:16.710
for years to come.

